  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nAn anomalous 594-mb heat ridge continues to hold firm over the \ncentral U.S. early this afternoon. Positioned along the northern\nperiphery of this heat dome, a weak surface low rests near \nSioux City, Iowa, with an associated warm front stretching \nsoutheastward through Columbia, Missouri toward Terre Haute, \nIndiana.\n\nAs weak shortwave energy ejects out of the Desert Southwest, it\nwill ride over the frontal boundary, offering sufficient \nsynoptic lift to drive scattered shower activity today. These\nshortwaves will also push the frontal system slightly north \nfrom tonight into Saturday. \n\nNear the warm front, pooling surface dewpoints in the lower 70s\nwill help support MLCAPE around 2000 J/kg, keeping the \npotential for an isolated thunderstorm in place. Poor mid-level \nlapse rates will limit any severe threat today. However, as the \nfront drifts northward on Saturday, increasing bulk shear (25\u201335\nkts) may compensate for the weak lapse rates, allowing for \noccasionally organized storms capable of producing localized \nsevere microburst winds and quarter-sized hail. Brief heavy\ndownpours resulting in 1\"/hr rates are a low (10-20%)\nprobability, but could accompany the more organized convection.\nThis potential is best highlighted by the HREF LPMM QPF, which\nhints at localized amounts up to 1.5\".\n\nBeyond the scattered showers and isolated storm potential \nthrough Saturday, the primary forecast difficulty centers on the\nsharp temperature contrast across the region. With the boundary\ndraped across central Illinois, locations to the north will be \ncapped in the 80s, whereas areas to the south will climb back \ninto the 90s with heat indices approaching 100 F. Outflow from \npassing precipitation could lead to sharp local variations in \ntemperatures through Saturday.\n\nBy Sunday, model guidance and ensemble suites exhibit high \nconfidence that a closed upper-level trough will track east \nacross the Plains and into the Mid-Mississippi Valley by Monday.\nThis system will dismantle the heat ridge and push the frontal \nboundary south and east of the forecast area. In its wake, \nexpansive high pressure will settle in under a blocking split-\nflow regime aloft, bringing a period of drier and significantly \ncooler conditions. Daytime highs from Tuesday through Friday are\nexpected to remain in the upper 60s to mid 70s.",
